"[2510.13161v1] Mirror Speculative Decoding: Breaking the Serial Barrier in LLM Inference (https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13161v1)\nciteturn3044view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.13161v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 15 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 11 Dec 2025 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Mirror Speculative Decoding: Breaking the Serial Barrier in LLM Inference\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Nikhil Bhendawade , cite7†Kumari Nishu , cite8†Arnav Kundu , cite9†Chris Bartels , cite10†Minsik Cho , cite11†Irina Belousova L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Mirror Speculative Decoding: Breaking the Serial Barrier in LLM Inference, by Nikhil Bhendawade and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Speculative decoding accelerates LLM inference by using a draft model to look ahead, but gains are capped by the cost of autoregressive draft generation: increasing draft size elevates acceptance rates but introduces additional latency overhead exacerbating the speed-accuracy tradeoff. Prior methods (Medusa, Hydra, EAGLE) partially reduce draft cost but either degrade acceptance or introduce overheads that limit scaling.\nL17: We present Mirror Speculative Decoding (Mirror-SD), an inference algorithm that breaks the latency-acceptance tradeoff. Mirror-SD launches branch-complete rollouts from early-exit signals in parallel with the target model's suffix and explicitly maps computation across heterogeneous accelerators (GPU and NPU) to exploit cross-device parallelism.\nL18: The draft speculates forward continuations for the target to verify, while the target simultaneously speculates correction paths for the draft, converting speculation into two complementary execution pipelines. To further cut draft latency without weakening acceptance semantics, we add speculative streaming so the draft emits multiple tokens per step.\nL19: This dual strategy of parallel heterogeneous execution plus multi-token speculative streaming pushes speculative decoding toward its ideal regime of high acceptance with low overhead. On SpecBench with server-scale models from 14B to 66B parameters, Mirror-SD delivers consistent end-to-end gains, achieving 2.8x-5.8x wall-time speedups across diverse tasks and a 30% average relative improvement over the strongest baseline, EAGLE3.\nL20: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite14†arXiv:2510.13161 [cs.CL]\nL22:    | (or cite15†arXiv:2510.13161v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL23:    | cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.13161†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Nikhil Bhendawade [cite17†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Wed, 15 Oct 2025 05:22:57 UTC (326 KB)\nL28: cite5†[v2] Thu, 11 Dec 2025 22:21:09 UTC (312 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Mirror Speculative Decoding: Breaking the Serial Barrier in LLM Inference, by Nikhil Bhendawade and 5 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite12†View PDF L37:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite18†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite19†view license L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CL\nL44: \nL45: cite20†< prev |   cite21†next > L46: \nL47: cite22†new | cite23†recent | cite24†2025-10 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite25†cs L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * c